**Amendment (Declared performance joins)** · `amend:devperf:registry-join` The reporting and join implementation is complete for the current suites through (`amend:harness:registry-join`), superseding the provisional location rule and pending join in (`amend:devperf:foundation-reporting`). The gates (`gate:devperf:foundation`) and (`gate:design:performance-foundation`) remain open for harness support, the claims step and tracing together; support is now implemented under (`amend:harness:foundation-closure`), while claims and tracing remain pending. Cases, suites, commands and gate spans carry their applicable budget references. Nextest suite envelopes use monotonic observer arrival offsets of suite start and completion, not the suite exec_time aggregate or a sum of case durations. Retained observations show interleaved binary events between those endpoints, rather than one completed binary block; these spans include observer transport and scheduling delay and do not claim exact engine timestamps. Doctest and CI envelopes likewise retain their observed elapsed spans. No envelope sums overlapping case durations; compilation remains separately visible and inside its enclosing command. Cargo-building gate steps and the nextest build use the incremental-build warning budget even when a run incurs cold work; cache state is not inferred from elapsed time. Build and aggregate misconfiguration findings follow the declared budget category, not operation names. An admitted register entry changes only its named operation's warning threshold; candidate or withdrawn entries grant no override. Reporter-contract probes inject noisy clock quality, incompatible cache states, overlapping spans and excess over an admitted budget, preserving supplied predicates and reporter exit status. These probes establish input handling, not measurement of a real clock, cache or execution order. The benchmark runner records its shared-worker clock quality and cache context; other collectors do not establish cache comparability.

**Amendment (Comparison evidence)** · `amend:devperf:comparison-evidence` The reporter appends comparison assessments to .lane/performance-assessments.jsonl separately from raw results and .lane/performance-findings.jsonl. Missing measurements or baselines, noisy clocks, incompatible cache states and unestablished comparisons are explicitly inconclusive; budget excess still warns and a completed functional result remains completed. Comparable regression evidence requires an explicitly compatible baseline with at least five samples; no gate run manufactures one. Baseline fields and original observations are retained, not replaced automatically. The support step provides benchmark workloads and smoke under (`rule:harness:benchmarks`); reviewed comparable baselines remain absent and their comparison is inconclusive rather than invented evidence. Doctest accounting uses the committed prior roster and refuses missing required execution just like nextest. Identity is the lexically normalized file path, item (or module for file documentation) and one-based example ordinal in source-line order within that item. Line numbers select the ordinal but are not identity; unrelated line shifts preserve names. Duplicate source positions and normalized identities are structural errors, and adding or removing an example still requires a reviewed roster change. Raw rustdoc names remain in retained evidence. The runner checks cargo-nextest exactly 0.9.149 before building or executing against its experimental stream; a different version is a named structural refusal. The owned warning path is implemented for current declared suites; property and benchmark support is now implemented, while the remaining holds are stated in (`amend:harness:matrix-evidence`).


**Amendment (Support and benchmark evidence)** · `amend:devperf:support-evidence` The support implementation now joins each RFC 8949 contract row and each named benchmark smoke execution by declared suite, implementation, tier, source and configuration under (`amend:harness:support-package`) and (`amend:harness:owned-benchmarks`). Property results retain their replay configuration under (`amend:harness:property-replay`). Fast Criterion measurements are inconclusive comparison evidence on a shared worker with no reviewed comparable baseline; correctness remains the only benchmark verdict predicate. Compilation uses the build budget, while workload execution retains its case budget and measured work units. Reviewed baseline replacement is never automated, and injected regressions preserve passing outcomes while emitting owned warnings and persistent findings under (`rule:devperf:nonverdict`).

**Amendment (Foundation gate remainder)** · `amend:devperf:foundation-remainder` The measured build, declared result joins and support evidence complete this step's mechanisms under (`amend:devperf:support-evidence`), not the whole of (`gate:devperf:foundation`) or (`gate:design:performance-foundation`). Their remaining foundation evidence includes the complete claim audit and tracing, together with the harness support now exercised under (`amend:harness:foundation-closure`). Neither performance gate is declared closed. Service, domain and client evidence remains held as applicable, and a passing smoke sample does not discharge reviewed comparable benchmark baselines.

**Amendment (Observation co-closure)** · `amend:devperf:observation-co-closure` The measured profile and tool decisions, register disposition, nonverdict reporter, current result joins, harness support and claims audit combine with (`amend:observe:foundation-evidence`) to support the implemented portions of (`gate:devperf:foundation`) and (`gate:design:performance-foundation`). This supersedes the claims and tracing remainder recorded earlier; it does not manufacture comparable benchmark baselines or change any duration into a verdict. The register reports scoped support rather than unrestricted closed gates. Real PostgreSQL and Elements service evidence: service implementer, provisioning slice; domain suites: assigned domain implementer, corresponding domain bite; client harnesses and actual exporter transport admission: client implementer, client slice. Decision-level evidence is not implicitly closed by these scoped gate rows.

**Amendment (Foundation closing measurements)** · `amend:devperf:closing-measurements` Under (`rule:devperf:comparison`), the retained samples below compare the pre-observation configuration and the current implementation on the same worker, profile and selected CPU at the lowest scheduling priority. The warmed pair runs one unmeasured invocation of each exact case immediately before its sample, with fresh processes and temporary roots. The selected CPU was 98 percent idle beforehand, but the shared node was not globally quiet; the recorded load and CPU context are premises, not a claim that scheduling had no effect. No publication-test regression was reproduced. The counted-repetition correction changes the matcher work between the historical and current configurations; this campaign does not change that engine or attribute its cost to observation. The historical tree predates the separate n=20 site, so its comparison is a test-only slice of the original aggregate preserving its five patterns, subject set, oracle and assertions; the unmodified aggregate is reported separately and is not a like-for-like comparator. The four requested current cases remain ordinary unit evidence with a 100 ms warning budget. The recorder/SDK/OTLP/transport witness is declared integration under (`amend:harness:case-level-scope`), with the ordinary 2,000 ms budget; its private in-crate placement does not make its composed evidence a unit. Earlier excesses remain findings, and no test predicate, retry policy or timeout changes. Single samples cannot establish the five-sample regression criterion; that comparison remains inconclusive.

